{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1109144-fae-blood-and-wraiths","id":1109144,"title":"Fae Blood and Wraiths","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-29T01:20:00-07:00","id":1109144,"title":"Fae Blood and Wraiths","content":"Hello all, <br /> So I've got one character in my campaign who's a euthanatos, obsessed with the dead, works in the morgue, got the Medium merit. And another with Irish Verbena heritage and Fae Blood merit. <br /> I'm not particularly familiar with either of these game lines, and especially uncertain about how to portray interactions with these other entities in a way that isn't dreadful. I have played a little changeling but that was 15 years ago. <br /> <br /> Does anyone have any pointers, or good examples they've seen of &quot;crossovers&quot; that don't suck? <br /> <br /> Many thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13587-eyrelliah","name":"Eyrelliah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T02:50:29-07:00","id":1109165,"title":"","content":"Wraiths can't be sensed without spirit unless they have the Embody Arcanos (arcanoi are the powers Wraith have)<br /> Wraiths have 10 health levels called corpus, and any physical attack done to them always does only 1 damage and makes them immaterial for a while, unable to be hit again for a few turns.<br /> <br /> What makes wraiths distinct from other spirits is their shadow, the personified dark side of a Wraith with its own desires. Think of it like a mage who has a split personality and the other personality is a nephandi (or sometimes a marauder)<br /> The shadow has subtle influences on the Wraith, but can take over and become dominant when the Wraith becomes weak or if they lose something which is incredibly important to them.<br /> <br /> Without Spirit magic, it's usually impossible to do anything other than 1 point of damage to a Wraith at a time. Prime and spirit can be used to drain a wraith of its Pathos (a wraiths power pool, used to fuel arcanos, only able to be refuelled if a Wraith can experience their passions, wraiths are pretty emotional beings after all).<br /> Doing Entropy magic on a Wraith will imidiately bring out the shadow, and the shadow gets stronger the longer it stays out. A Wraith that is permanently dominated by their shadow is known as a spectre.<br /> <br /> Wraiths have tenuous connections to our world (known to them as the skinlands). These connections are usually via fetters; objects, people, or places which held significance to the Wraith in life. These fetters are what keep them tethered to the shadowlands, and provide them a relatively safe place to slumber. When a fetter is destroyed, the Wraith experiences a harrowing by which they usually becoming weaker and their shadows become stronger.<br /> <br /> Losing all corpus and willpower also results in a shadow strengthening harrowing.<br /> <br /> Quick note about the Dark Umbra.<br /> <br /> It's separated into layers, each one further removed from the skinlands.<br /> <br /> First is the Shadowlands, separated by the Shroud (the Gauntlet). When a Mage enters the penumbra and has the videre mortum (as most Euthanatos would) they will see glimpses of this world, but not coherently. To properly travel to the shadowlands you must either die or perform the Aghama Sojurn rite. This is a right of passage for all Euthanatos mages for one cannot give death without experiencing it first hand.<br /> <br /> The shadowlands is a world of decay and memory. That which has been destroyed in life has a reflection here. It's form changing to resemble aspects of how it is remembered. A demolished school may look like a tall and imposing prison, while a hospital may seem both terrifying and strangely comforting. As memories fade in time so too will these places, and as such almost everything is decaying to some degree.<br /> <br /> Beyond/beneath the shadowlands is the tempest, a roaring spiral of chaos where memories go to die. The tempest can be anything at any point, a swirling storm of broken glass, a downpour of blood and pillow cases, a scene from somebody's childhood, the only thing that's known is time is immaterial in the tempest and harrowings are more likely. The more Entropic resonance one has, the &quot;heavier&quot; they will be, pulling them further away from the shadowlands and deeper into the tempest. Wraiths who lose all their fetters are trapped here.<br /> <br /> There are areas of stability in the tempest, and wraiths have been able to form societies in such places. This includes such sprawling masses as the city of Stygia and the empire of the Yellow Spring, though unless you're planning a Wraith centric storyline you need not know more than this about such places.<br /> <br /> At the centre of the tempest, or perhaps the very bottom (direction loses all meaning here) lies the labyrinth. A horrible place where spectres and their dark masters rule, plot, and gather, waiting for then next maelstrom so they may attack both Stygia, the yellow springs, and the shadowlands. If the tempest is where memories go to die, then the labyrinth is where they go to rot. Nightmares take form and wraiths that enter are at constant risk of turning spectre the longer they stay.<br /> <br /> At the centre of the labyrinth lies the gaping maw of oblivion itself. A great hole from which nothing ever returns. It pulls at all who enter the tempest, slowly drawing you in. To face oblivion is to face the ultimate unknown.<br /> <br /> That ought to be enough to give you cool ideas for your campaign.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":12193,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","user":"Saikou","date":". 06-30-2017, 07:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T04:45:46-07:00","id":1109182,"title":"","content":"I have some question. If i may. How do they move? I mean in out world, or in Shadowlands. Can they see our world and if how?<br /> Because i read many necromancers (especially vampire clan Giovanni but i am sure some mage too) use them like spies but i am not sure how that even work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5536-arkilijo","name":"Arkilijo","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"46"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T06:49:31-07:00","id":1109220,"title":"","content":"Yes, they can sense the world of the living trough the Shroud, in fact, their senses are very good. When Wraiths are in the Shadowlads, they see our world as a decayed shadow, but they see it. They have special two sights:<br /> <br /> Deathsight it's the way they see everything in our world, everything looks decayed because they see the entropy slowly tearing things away, this sight allows a wraith to have an understanding of how material things can be destroyed, and allows them to detect sickness or other weaknesses on living beings.<br /> <br /> Life Sight allows them to sense the life force of the living, this allows to detect the living very easily when they travel to the Underworld, and also see the living very clearly in the skinlands (in our side of the Shroud). I believe that vampires can be detected this way because they don't have the same lifeforce as humans (but I'm not sure).<br /> One of the advantages of Agama Sojourn over other forms of travel it's that it hides to some extent your life force (but wraiths can make a roll to detect it).<br /> Also, the few Wraiths that know that the Middle Umbral Penumbra exists can see it using Life Sight and a roll of Perception<br /> <br /> Plus, their senses can pierce through Obfuscation from 1 to 5 automatically.<br /> <br /> A Wraith can't influence, damage or move stuff in the material world from the Shadowlads without Poltergeist or other Arcanoi, but at the same time they can't just go trough a wall or a solid object that exists in the material world, the Wraith needs to loose a point of Corpus to become immaterial and pass trough solid stuff.<br /> <br /> A wall that exists on their side of the Shroud (like a Relic created from the memories of a demolished building) blocks them like a wall would block a human being. You need Spirit to harm a Wraith because you need the damage to pass the Shroud, not because they're immune to physical damage. If you're in Agama you can punch the wraith causing normal damage, and he can punch you back.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 06-29-2017, 09:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T07:53:20-07:00","id":1109240,"title":"","content":"Getting into how a mage would go about dealing with Wraiths, the latter have three interests that the former can help with (and thus use as trade for whatever task you need the ghost to do):<br /> <br /> <br /> -Resolving unfinished business and protecting the wraith's earthly interests. If the wraith was murdered, they may want that death avenged. Or the wraith might have loved ones that they want to protect and provide for, which the ghost has a limited ability to do on their own. Another wraith might have been a writer, needing the help of a living person to finish their magnum opus and get it published. Generally important matters to the wraith as an individual, that they can't accomplish because they are dead.<br /> <br /> <br /> -Finding their Fetters, and protecting or destroying them. Among the dead, it is uncertain if there is an afterlife, or if being a wraith trapped in the Shadowlands is all the existence they get after death. The party line of the Hierarchy (which may or may not still exist in your game) was that there was no afterlife beyond the Shadowlands; an idea they perpetuate in order to protect their own autocracy. As such, a ghost who doesn't believe there's another afterlife to look forward to - or who simply doesn't want to leave this world yet - will want to have the Fetters anchoring them protected. Conversely, a wraith who believes they have an Eternal Reward waiting for them will desire to systematically sever the ties that bind them, and will therefore ask necromancers and mediums to obtain and destroy their Fetters (whether immediately or at the time of the wraith's choosing, when they're ready to go).<br /> <br /> Since mages are some of the most powerful folks around, they are well qualified to find and safeguard Fetters, or to magically sever the ties a wraith has to them. A mage can even use magick to turn themselves or objects into added Fetters, if the wraith desires more protection. Of course, mages are also capable of using Fetters against wraiths; for this reason, a wraith will be justifiably reluctant to give a mage access to one. It's also why a mage's reputation among the dead is so important; if a wraith gets mistreated by a mage willing to take advantage of her, she's liable to tell every other wraith she knows, if only to spite the mage. So good conduct is important.<br /> <br /> <br /> -Fueling the wraith's supply of Pathos. A ghost's power is fueled by absorbing emotional energy. Ergo, it's often possible to get a wraith to do what the living person wants, if they allow the wraith to enjoy moments of human emotion vicariously. This might mean starting a raucous party, a bar fight, a poetry slam, a bit of passionate love-making, a bout of scaring the crap out of random people, or a viewing of a sad film. Anything to get the emotional juices flowing can be helpful, if the ghost is allowed to park herself on the other side of the Gauntlet and lap those juices up. It can present its own problems, but it's also one of the most straight-forward ways to pay a ghost for services rendered. It's so basic, even the unAwakened can pull it off.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T13:17:30-07:00","id":1109369,"title":"","content":"Thanks all, I've got an idea how to interact with Wraiths now. <br /> On the fae side of things, I've been revising how they work and what they care about, and since the players are going to glastonbury festival as an end to their intro arc there'll be a good chance for the appropriate character to see some weird shit. <br /> What I'd like to do is have some interactions that are not just transactional (you help x supernatural thing and learn a bit about them) and possibly leave future plot ideas open. <br /> Perhaps with the fae, something multi-layered - getting dragged into an ongoing storyline that puts their morals on the line? Always the best thing to do it seems. <br /> I'm thinking a teenager who is extremely worrying in their behaviour to lead to some conflict about what is and isn't ok - fae don't really ascribe to our norms about appropriate behaviour for different age groups. <br /> I don't want to just go to &quot;teenage groupie feeding off the glamour of a musician while being exploited&quot; though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13587-eyrelliah","name":"Eyrelliah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}